# Pipeline

A **flow** is orchestration, not computation — it *sequences* other units by name.
`step` runs stages in order; `from:` threads one stage's output into the next;
`parallel` runs independent work at once. The real work stays in [[Enrich]] and
[[Score]]; the flow just wires them.

flow :pipeline do
  step :seed do
    query(Lead) |> Enum.filter(&(&1.status == :new))
  end

  # each CRM lookup is independent, so fan out
  step :enrich, from: :seed do
    parallel leads, max_concurrency: 8 do
      lead -> Enrich.enrich(lead)
    end
  end

  # pure and fast — keep it sequential
  step :score, from: :enrich do
    Enum.map(enriched, fn lead -> %Lead{lead | score: Score.score(lead), status: :scored} end)
  end

  step :rank, from: :score do
    Enum.sort_by(scored, &{&1.score, &1.revenue}, :desc)
  end
end

`parallel` is for **independence**, not speed for its own sake — fan out I/O-bound
stages, leave cheap pure work in a plain `step`. Calling `Enrich.enrich/1` here *is*
importing that unit — the gate records a dataflow edge, so `work why :pipeline` shows
the wiring. Next: [[Pricing under a sandbox]].
